### 3. Cache the HTML

Wrap the retrieval and extraction of the HTML in a cache layer, so that you can
access the content again without needing to retrieve it using the API.

Caching reduces the number of requests to the API, and can help you stay within
the [rate limit][rate-limiting].

Each of the following code snippets uses a simple in-memory cache and a 1 hour expiry
time.

<!-- markdownlint-disable MD034 MD037 -->
<div class="govuk-tabs" data-module="govuk-tabs">
  <ul class="govuk-tabs__list">
    <li class="govuk-tabs__list-item govuk-tabs__list-item--selected">
      <a class="govuk-tabs__tab" href="#ruby-cache">
        Ruby on Rails
      </a>
    </li>
    <li class="govuk-tabs__list-item">
      <a class="govuk-tabs__tab" href="#python-cache">
        Python
      </a>
    </li>
    <li class="govuk-tabs__list-item">
      <a class="govuk-tabs__tab" href="#node-cache">
        Node.js
      </a>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="govuk-tabs__panel" id="ruby-cache">
    <h5 class="govuk-heading-l">Ruby on Rails</h5>
    <% code("ruby") do %>
require "rest-client"

tax_help = Rails.cache.fetch("govuk/tax-help", expires_in: 1.hour) do
  api_response = RestClient.get(
  "https://www.gov.uk/api/content/tax-help"
  )
  content_data = JSON.parse(api_response.body)
  content_data.dig("details", "body")
end
    <% end %>
  </div>
  <div class="govuk-tabs__panel govuk-tabs__panel--hidden" id="python-cache">
    <h5 class="govuk-heading-l">Python</h5>
    <% code("python") do %>
import time, requests

_cache = {"data": None, "expires": 0}

def get_tax_help():
    if time.time() > _cache["expires"]:
        api_response = requests.get(
          "https://www.gov.uk/api/content/tax-help"
        )
        content_data = api_response.json()
        _cache["data"] = (
            content_data.get("details", {})
            .get("body", "")
        )
        _cache["expires"] = time.time() + 3600
    return _cache["data"]
    <% end %>
  </div>
  <div class="govuk-tabs__panel govuk-tabs__panel--hidden" id="node-cache">
    <h5 class="govuk-heading-l">Node.js</h5>
    <% code("javascript") do %>
let cache = { body: null, expires: 0 };

async function getTaxHelp() {
    if (Date.now() > cache.expires) {
        const apiResponse = await fetch(
          "https://www.gov.uk/api/content/tax-help"
        );
        const contentData = await apiResponse.json();
        cache.body = contentData.details?.body || "";
        cache.expires = Date.now() + 3600000;
    }
    return `${cache.body}`;
}
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
<!-- markdownlint-enable MD034 MD037 -->

### 4. Add the HTML to your application

Now you can add the returned HTML to your application as needed. Here, we will
add the content to a simple web page snippet with an `h1` heading.

You also need to provide the CSS needed to style the HTML in your application,
but this is omitted in the example.

